 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "shropshire_iron_bridge",
   "title": "The Iron Bridge",
   "text": "The first major bridge in the world made of cast iron, cast by Abraham Darby III at Coalbrookdale and opened in 1781. Nobody had built in iron before, so its 378 tonnes of parts were joined like a timber frame, with dovetails and wedges. It gave the town, and a whole revolution, its name."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "shropshire_gorge",
   "title": "Ironbridge Gorge",
   "text": "Geologists think the gorge was cut at the end of the last Ice Age, when meltwater burst out of a lake dammed by ice and carved a new course for the Severn. Coal, iron ore, limestone and clay all lay close together here, and in the 1700s the gorge became the busiest industrial valley on Earth. It was made a World Heritage Site in 1986."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 10,
   "plaque": "shropshire_old_furnace",
   "title": "Darby's Old Furnace",
   "text": "In 1709 Abraham Darby I smelted iron here using coke, baked from coal, instead of charcoal from trees. Charcoal was running out with the forests; coal was not. Cheap iron poured out of Coalbrookdale for pots, rails, steam engines and, in time, the Iron Bridge. The furnace is still here, under a glass roof."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "shropshire_pig_iron",
   "title": "Pig Iron",
   "text": "Molten iron from the furnace ran into a channel in the sand floor, with lots of little moulds branching off it. Ironworkers thought it looked like a row of piglets feeding from a sow, so the bars were called pigs, and the metal pig iron."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "shropshire_wrekin",
   "title": "The Wrekin",
   "text": "A lone hill, 407 metres high, that every Salopian can see from home. Iron Age people built a hill fort on top. To go 'all round the Wrekin' is to take the long way round, and the old Shropshire toast is 'to all friends round the Wrekin'. The red light on top is a television mast."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "shropshire_guildhall",
   "title": "Much Wenlock Guildhall",
   "text": "A timber-framed guildhall of 1540, built over an open market space where the stocks and the whipping post once stood. The town council still meets upstairs, without the whipping post."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 42,
   "plaque": "shropshire_priory",
   "title": "Wenlock Priory",
   "text": "St Milburga ran an abbey here in the 600s, and Cluniac monks rebuilt it as a great priory after the Normans came. Henry VIII closed it in 1540; the ruins, and some magnificent topiary, are what is left."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 43,
   "plaque": "shropshire_olympian",
   "title": "The Wenlock Olympian Games",
   "text": "In 1850 a Much Wenlock doctor, William Penny Brookes, started the Wenlock Olympian Games, with running, jumping, cricket, quoits and tilting at the ring on horseback. Pierre de Coubertin came to see them in 1890, and six years later he started the modern Olympic Games in Athens. The Wenlock Games still run every July."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 50,
   "plaque": "shropshire_wenlock_edge",
   "title": "Wenlock Edge",
   "text": "A limestone scarp running for about thirty kilometres, made of the reefs of a warm tropical sea some 425 million years ago; look closely and the stone is full of corals and shells. A. E. Housman wrote 'On Wenlock Edge the wood's in trouble' in A Shropshire Lad, and the wood still is, on windy days."
  },
  {
   "x": 64,
   "y": 43,
   "plaque": "shropshire_blists_hill",
   "title": "Blists Hill Victorian Town",
   "text": "An open-air museum built round real blast furnaces, a brickworks and a canal, with a whole Victorian town of shops, a bank, a pub and a foundry, staffed by people in Victorian dress. You can change your money into pounds, shillings and pence at the bank and spend it in the sweet shop."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 50,
   "plaque": "shropshire_tar_tunnel",
   "title": "The Tar Tunnel",
   "text": "In 1786 miners digging a canal tunnel at Coalport struck a spring of natural bitumen, black tar oozing out of the rock. They sold it as medicine and for caulking boats. It still seeps from the brick walls today."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 56,
   "plaque": "shropshire_coalport",
   "title": "Coalport China",
   "text": "Downriver at Coalport, from the 1790s, the works made fine porcelain painted with flowers and birds, fired in bottle kilns by the canal. Coalport china was on the tables of kings, and the old works is now a museum."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "WEST: Shrewsbury. NORTH: Coalbrookdale, the Wrekin. EAST over the Iron Bridge: Ironbridge, Blists Hill. SOUTH: Much Wenlock."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "CORACLE RACE. Eight pairs of buoys down the Severn, round boat, one paddle. Mind the spin. The Iron Bridge is the finish."
  }
 ],
